oh the best beer in the world's made in  America there's no question about  there's more creativity and more  integrity and more variety in America  than the rest of the world combined  my name is Fritz Maytag and I'm the  owner of anchor Brewing Company I call  myself president and brew master or  owner and brew master I used to hang out  at the Old Spaghetti Factory in fact the  I went there long before I knew anything  about the brewery in the summer of 57  that's when I first met the owner Fred  ku  I used to later I used to hang out there  it was the equivalent of an Englishman's  local the pub that you go to every  evening maybe before after dinner and  before bed you go down and have a beer  with your friends or - so I was thinking  Anchor Steam and the owner came up to me  and said you if you haven't ever been to  the brewery you should go because you'd  like it for its end and they're closing  they're bankrupt and they're going to  close on Friday this week you better get  down there I did right away but he sent  me down there and indeed I just fell in  love I mean within a few hours I had  decided I learned that it could be  purchased the majority ownership and I  had committed to do that I bought it for  a song they were in desperate financial  trouble but I fell in love with it and I  bought and then quickly realized that  what we needed was not so much sales a  little money but then but more care in  terms of brewing and so we went into a  program where we I studied brewing  I studied what we had I figured out what  we needed so promptly the beer was  better and then gradually it became not  only better but also more traditional  they had begun taking shortcuts the way  so many breweries were doing in those  days and we went back to the most  fundamental traditional type of brewing  philosophy and so we had that  combination that I call the micro  brewing  Renaissance which was a combination of  extremely traditional attitude towards  materials and process absolute basic  old-fashioned brewing with pure purity  and simplicity but combined with modern  food processing technology and equipment  you know the 60s weren't caused by the  60s they were caused by ideas and some  of those were very good ideas that have  always been true and always will be true  and of those among those good ideas are  to be open-minded and to value  creativity and to try new things and to  not be afraid and things like that  my favorite 60s story is that I lived on  the just on the other side of the Haight  as we call it the the zone where all  this stuff was wildly happening and the  brewery was on one side and my house was  on the other my family for two children  and my wife lived up on the hill just  above the Haight and I had to drive  through the Haight every night but I  used to get up samples that are they're  green fluffy leafy things and at one  point I realized we are desperate for  publicity and there'd be nothing better  than getting arrested for having what  would look like something green and  leafy in my car and turn out to be hops  you know false arrests Brewer is  arrested for hops and those days there  weren't any little breweries I mean  nobody had ever heard of a guy owning a  brewery let alone driving home with hops  I put hops on this passenger seat of my  car I had a little Porsche and I drove  home with the hops on the seat for Oh  back and forth for at least a week  hoping to get arrested well you couldn't  get arrested in the Haight Ashbury in  those days  the whole food runners are I call it the  food Renaissance that's taking place in  America that started primarily in  California and has spread around the  world and of course it was borrowing  from different places of the food  Renaissance has been a worldwide  phenomenon but it certainly has been  especially in American phenomenon I have  thought about it a bit and I think there  are several reasons one we've had a  fantastic economy so there's been a  tremendous ability for what used to be  just modestly endowed people to have  what they wanted and so that's had a  huge effect I would also argue that in  America we have not just political  freedom and religious freedom and  economic freedom we have what I call  cultural freedom a friend of mine says  in Italy they eat Italian food in  America we need any kind of food we want  in the 60s and in the early 70s I never  dreamed that there would be any other  little breweries like ours  I tried to persuade some of my friends  who did own little family breweries in  rural places to try to do what we were  doing but I was never successful only  later to my amazement when the some of  the new little breweries sprang up did I  read it I come to the realization that  what we had done was possible for others  to do I thought we had done something so  unusual so difficult frankly for us that  the chances of anyone ever copying us  were were just zero I certainly was  disabused of that promptly we now have  1500 they say 1500 new little breweries  I heard the other day that there are 300  that are coming on stream soon so we  could have 2,000 new little  or is it some unbelievable phenomena I  mean for me it's just absolutely  mind-boggling I decided to brew an ale  just once and it was in this early  spring of 1975 and I was at the same  time aware that the Bicentennial was  coming up 1776 but I'm a guy who liked I  don't like to join the crowd I like to  get out ahead and be a little different  and so I thought Geus must be some way  we could do a Bicentennial early ahead  of the rest of the crowd oh I thought 75  hardly a man is now alive who remembers  that famous day and year and the  midnight ride of Paul Revere and I  thought that's it and it was maybe it  was March and I thought April 18th  so we brewed the first brew of Liberty  ale on April 18th with the whole concept  in mind and we put on the label a brewed  on the 18th of April in 75 or something  like that and I chose the name with  great care and knowledge and patriotism  and  I'm an exceptional I think the whole  American story is just unbelievable so I  thought that would be a fun name also  Albion in modern times considered to be  a British specialty I thought we would  brew an ale better than anything in  Britain then we'd call it Liberty and  we'd win on our all accounts and so we  did that and the ale was terrible I just  didn't like it at all I didn't want to  sell it I wanted to dump it  then we brewed a Christmas Ale which was  another condition that I was very  familiar with that once upon a time ales  beers were brewed for holidays  especially religious festivals and that  was a vital part of medieval life to  brew an ale in the village for the  festival or for the wedding or for  whatever so with great delight we brewed  a Christmas Ale again just a few hundred  cases and we gave it to our friends and  we sold some and then we brewed  Christmas ale every year for years  different every year we didn't have  capacity to brew a regularly but in 1983  we had moved into this brewery almost 20  years after I started now we had  capacity and the 1983 Christmas sale  different each year but that year's  Christmas sale was a smash success and  we suddenly thought a hot that's it  that's Liberty ale we'll redo Liberty  Ale  so today's Liberty Ale is exactly the  brew that we made for Christmas 1983 I  like to tell about what it's like out  there in the trade when you're selling  beer which is hard work and if you've  ever pounded the pavement you know what  Schule there means and at the end of the  day when the beer salesman finds a place  a watering hole to settle down and real  the guy from a curse it's down in  Tommy's tavern and guess who's there  the guy from Sierra Nevada and guess  what they do they have a beer together  and after about one beer they're no  longer arch enemies they're two beer  guys and I like to think that that's  part of why the whole beer trade is a  brotherhood of sorts and I also like to  ask I wonder what the shoe guys do at  the end of the day there's something  about beer guys so yeah beer guys are  real

